 NSW and the Supplier acknowledge and agree to the commitments set out in the Statement of Business Ethics available on our website. The Statement should be read in conjunction with the NSW Government Supplier Code of Conduct.The content in Annexures form a part of this Contract unless expressly stated otherwise. Any case studies or award submissions must be approved prior by Destination NSW before use or lodgement respectively. Such award submissions must be jointly drafted and lodged with Destination NSW.  The Supplier may have access to use of Destination NSW\'s facilities, equipment and/or systems as required for the performance of the Services, except as otherwise agreed between the Supplier and Destination NSW. The Supplier acknowledges and agrees that where it is providing services on behalf of Destination NSW or exercising the functions of Destination NSW, the Supplier and its employees are public officials for the purposes of the Public Interest Disclosures Act 2022 (PID Act).The Supplier will investigate and implement sustainability measures where reasonably possible. Such sustainability measures may include recycling, use of digital rather than printed collateral, and use of public transport.\tDestination NSW and the Supplier acknowledge and agree to the commitments set out in the Statement of Business Ethics available on our website. The Statement should be read in conjunction with the NSW Government Supplier Code of Conduct.The content in Annexures form a part of this Contract unless expressly stated otherwise. Any case studies or award submissions must be approved prior by Destination NSW before use or lodgement respectively. Such award submissions must be jointly drafted and lodged with Destination NSW.  The Supplier may have access to use of Destination NSW\'s facilities, equipment and/or systems as required for the performance of the Services, except as otherwise agreed between the Supplier and Destination NSW. The Supplier acknowledges and agrees that where it is providing services on behalf of Destination NSW or exercising the functions of Destination NSW, the Supplier and its employees are public officials for the purposes of the Public Interest Disclosures Act 2022 (PID Act).The Supplier will investigate and implement sustainability measures where reasonably possible. Such sustainability measures may include recycling, use of digital rather than printed collateral, and use of public transport.\tDestination NSW and the Supplier acknowledge and agree to the commitments set out in the Statement of Business Ethics available on our website. The Statement should be read in conjunction with the NSW Government Supplier Code of Conduct.The content in Annexures form a part of this Contract unless expressly stated otherwise. Any case studies or award submissions must be approved prior by Destination NSW before use or lodgement respectively. Such award submissions must be jointly drafted and lodged with Destination NSW.  The Supplier may have access to use of Destination NSW\'s facilities, equipment and/or systems as required for the performance of the Services, except as otherwise agreed between the Supplier and Destination NSW. The Supplier acknowledges and agrees that where it is providing services on behalf of Destination NSW or exercising the functions of Destination NSW, the Supplier and its employees are public officials for the purposes of the Public Interest Disclosures Act 2022 (PID Act).The Supplier will investigate and implement sustainability measures where reasonably possible. Such sustainability measures may include recycling, use of digital rather than printed collateral, and use of public transport.\nEXECUTION:\nSIGNED for the Supplier in accordance with section [126 / 127] of the Corporations Act 2001:\tSIGNED for the Supplier in accordance with section [126 / 127] of the Corporations Act 2001:\n________________________Signature of Director/Authorised Representative_________Date_________________________________Full Name (Printed) \t________________________Signature Director/Secretary/Authorised Representative_________Date_________________________________Full Name (Printed)\nSIGNED for Destination NSW by an authorised representative:\tSIGNED for Destination NSW by an authorised representative:\n________________________Signature _________Date_________________________________Full Name (Printed)\t\nCONTRACT STANDARD TERMS AND CONDITIONS\nGoods and Services\nFrom the Commencement Date until the Completion Date, the Supplier is engaged to undertake, supply and provide the goods and/or services, duties and outcomes set out in Item 3 and Item 4 of the Contract Schedule (?Goods and/or Services?). In exchange for the Goods and/or Services Destination NSW will pay the Supplier the Contract Price as set out in Item 6 of the Contract Schedule upon receipt of a valid invoice as set out in Item 7. \nEach party states that:\nit has full power and authority to execute, delivery and perform its obligations under this Contract;\nthis Contract is legally binding on and enforceable against it;\nit will comply with all applicable laws in the performance of its obligations under this Contract;\nits signatories are properly authorised; and\nit can enter into this Contract without breaching any other contract or legal obligation to anyone else.\nDestination NSW will reimburse the Supplier for out-of-pocket expenses in connection with Services, if any have been so identified for reimbursement in the invoice, provided that the Supplier has obtained prior written approval to incur such expenses at Destination NSW?s expense before they are incurred and valid tax invoices and receipts are provided for reconciliation purposes in accordance with Item 7. \nThe Supplier must provide the Goods and/or Services in accordance with any Milestones or Delivery Dates set out in Item 5 of the Contract Schedule. \nWhere the Supplier is supplying Goods to Destination NSW it:\nmust ensure and warrant that title to the Goods passes to Destination NSW on the Delivery Date free from encumbrances and all other adverse interests;\nacknowledges that all risk in the Goods will remain the Supplier\'s until the Goods are installed or delivered to the satisfaction of Destination NSW; and\nwarrants that the goods will be: \nfree from defects in materials or workmanship; \nof merchantable quality; \nfit for purpose; and,\ncarry any applicable manufacturer?s warranty which will pass to Destination NSW.\nThe Supplier and any Key Person/s must not unreasonably refuse a request by Destination NSW to inspect and test the Goods prior to acceptance of delivery of the Goods. Destination NSW is entitled to reject any Goods which are not manufactured or delivered in accordance with the terms of this Contract (which includes a failure to comply with the warranties contained in clause 5 (c) of this Contract). Destination NSW is not deemed to have accepted any Goods until the latter of:\nthe expiration of a reasonable time for inspection of the Goods following delivery and installation; or\nany latent defect in the Goods becoming apparent. \nIf any Goods are rejected by Destination NSW under clause 6, title to and risk in those Goods automatically re-vests in the Supplier. At the Supplier\'s sole expense, it is responsible for the collection and removal of the rejected Goods. Destination NSW may, in its sole discretion, require the Supplier to replace any rejected Goods, or reimburse the costs arising from having to acquire replacement Goods from a third party, without prejudice to any of the other rights or remedies Destination NSW may have under this Contract or at law. \nWhere the Supplier is providing Services to Destination NSW, the Supplier and any Key Person/s must:\nperform the Services in accordance with best industry practice. Destination NSW relies on the Supplier, as the Supplier of the Services, to know what the best industry practice is;\ncomply with the laws, standards and policies that apply to the Services;\nperform the Services with the due care, skill and diligence expected of a professional service supplier and in a proper and workmanlike manner; \nperform the Services in a cost-effective manner consistent with the required level of quality of performance; and\nensure that any equipment used in delivering the Services is suitable and in good, safe and serviceable order and continuously maintained or repaired so that it remains in such order.\nThe Supplier must notify Destination NSW immediately if it anticipates that there may be a delay in the provision of the Goods and/or Services. Any request for an extension of time to deliver the Goods and/or Services requires Destination NSW?s approval, which will not be unreasonably withheld. \nThe Supplier agrees that any work performed for Destination NSW will be deemed to fall within the Services for the Contract Price set out in Item 6 of the Contract Schedule. Should the Supplier wish to perform work believed to be outside the scope of the Services, it must seek written approval from Destination NSW before proceeding with any such work. Such approval may be withheld by Destination NSW in its sole discretion. \nTerm\nThis Contract will commence on the Commencement Date identified in Item 1 and will continue until the Completion Date identified in Item 1, unless renewed in accordance with clause 12.   \nThe Parties agree that Destination NSW may elect to renew this Contract for the Option Period(s) set out in Item 1 of the Contract Schedule, subject to the consent of the Supplier. \nIf Destination NSW elects to exercise an option to renew, Destination NSW will provide, within 30 days before the expiration of the Completion Date, notice in writing to the Supplier and the Contract Price Review mechanism set out in Item 8 of the Contract Schedule will apply. Unless otherwise agreed in writing between the parties (email is sufficient), all other terms and conditions set out in this Contract will remain in full force and effect.  \nInvoice and Tax\nThe Supplier must submit a tax invoice at the timeframes set out in Item 7 of the Contract Schedule. \nDestination NSW may withhold or apply a set-off against any tax invoice submitted by the Supplier if Destination NSW is not satisfied, acting reasonably, that the Goods and/or Services have been provided in accordance with the terms and conditions of this Contract. \nOther than as provided in this Contract, all taxes, duties and charges and taxation obligations imposed or levied in connection to the Contract Price (including, without limitation, making appropriate tax deductions and payments in respect of any Key Person/s as applicable) will be borne by the Supplier. \nIf GST is applicable under or in connection with this Contract, any reference in this clause to a term defined or used in the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (Cth) (?Act?) is, unless the context indicates otherwise, a reference to that term as defined or used in the Act. \nAny amount referred to in this Contract except as indicated otherwise, is expressed on a GST exclusive basis. The Supplier takes all responsibility for determining whether GST is applicable to it and Destination NSW will not be liable whatsoever, for upholding the Supplier\'s requirements and obligations under the Act. \nIf GST is payable under or in connection with this Contract, the consideration to be provided for the supply (unless it is expressly stated to include GST) is increased by an amount equal to the GST exclusive consideration multiplied by the rate at which GST is imposed in respect of the supply. \nTermination \nDestination NSW may at any time terminate this Contract for convenience by giving the Supplier 30 days? notice in writing, such termination being effective upon expiration of the 30-day period. Destination NSW will not be liable for any payments to the Supplier over and above the value of the payment due and payable for Goods provided or Services performed up to the date of termination under this clause. \nEither party may terminate this Contract if the other commits a material breach of this Contract which is either not capable of being remedied or which the party committing the breach has not remedied within seven (7) (or such larger number as agreed in writing) days of receiving a written notice requiring remedy from the other party. \nDestination NSW may terminate this Contract where the Supplier becomes bankrupt or stops, suspends or threatens to stop payment of all or a class of its debts, or where the Supplier appoints an administrator or liquidator.\nUpon termination of this Contract, any property belonging to Destination NSW which is in the possession of the Supplier and its Key Person/s must be immediately returned to Destination NSW.\nWhere this Contract is terminated by Destination NSW under clauses 21 or 22, Destination NSW may withhold any or all of the fees payable to the Supplier in exchange for the Services (other than fees for Goods or Services provided prior to the date of termination). Any fees prepaid by Destination NSW to the Supplier for Goods or Services not delivered to Destination NSW by the termination date are to be refunded to Destination NSW no later than 10 business days after the termination date.    \nIf there is any actual or threatened conduct which is or may be a breach of this Contract, both parties acknowledge that damages may be inadequate compensation for such a breach. Either party will be entitled to apply to any court of competent jurisdiction for interim or permanent injunctive relief or both, restraining the other party from committing any breach or threatened breach of this Contract. Such rights and remedies will be cumulative and in addition to any other rights or remedies which either party may be entitled to at law or in equity. \nForce Majeure\nA party (?Affected Party?) will not be liable for any failure or delay in the performance of its obligations under this Contract to the extent that such failure or delay is caused, directly or indirectly by any of the following circumstances (?Force Majeure Circumstance?):\nfire, flood, earthquake, or acts of God;\nstrikes or industrial action (but not strikes or industrial action by any employees of Destination NSW or the Supplier); and\nacts of war, pandemic, epidemic, terrorism, riots, civil disorders or rebellions or revolutions in Australia. \nThe Affected Party must:\npromptly advise the other party of the details of the Force Majeure Circumstance, and its likely effect on the Affected Party?s obligations under this Contract; and\ntake all steps reasonably necessary to recommence performance and minimise the delay caused by the Force Majeure Circumstance. \nIf the Services cannot be rearranged so that they are conducted, the Supplier will refund to Destination NSW within 10 business days of receiving notice from Destination NSW any unspent portion of the payments and no further payments will be made. \nIndemnity\nThe Supplier must indemnify Destination NSW against any loss, action, proceedings, demand, prosecution, judgment, damage, cost, expense, fine, penalty or liability claimed against or incurred by Destination NSW in relation to:\npersonal injury or damage to property arising out of:\na wilful misconduct (being an act or omission which was a deliberate and wrongful act or omission or involved reckless disregard or wanton indifference to the likely consequences) or any negligent act or omission by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, to provide the Goods or Services; or\nany breach by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s of this Contract (including but not limited to any loss resulting from the Supplier not being registered for GST and ABN purposes);\nbreach by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, of applicable Privacy and Data Protection Laws or confidentiality obligations; or\na material breach of the Contract by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, or infringement of Destination NSW?s intellectual property rights by the Supplier or its Key Peron/s; and,\nthe Supplier and/or its Key Person/s claiming that they are Destination NSW\'s employee, including claims for wages, superannuation, public holidays, annual leave, parental leave, personal carer s leave, long service leave, redundancy or termination of employment or any other employment entitlement. \nThe indemnity in this clause 29 will not apply to the extent that the relevant loss, action, proceedings, demand, prosecution, judgment, damage, cost, expense, fine, penalty or liability is caused or contributed to by Destination NSW.  \nKey Persons\nIf any Key Person/s are named at Item 2 of the Contract Schedule, then the Supplier must ensure that those Key Person/s are fully engaged in the Services on behalf of the Supplier. If any Key Person is unavailable, then the Supplier must notify Destination NSW and provide, at the earliest opportunity, replacements with comparable skill and experience, subject to written approval by Destination NSW. The Suppler acknowledges and agrees that Destination NSW may, in its absolute discretion, reject any proposed replacement(s) of one or more Key Person/s and that where this occurs, the Supplier must provide an alternate person with comparable skill and experience approved by Destination NSW of as soon as possible.\nConfidentiality, Personal Information and GIPA\nSubject to the Government Information (Public Access) Act 2009 (NSW) (GIPA Act), the Supplier and its Key Person/s acknowledge the importance of confidentiality in all aspects of Destination NSW?s operations and agree to keep all Confidential Information in absolute confidence and not to disclose any such information to any person without our prior written consent both during and after the term of this Contract. \n"Confidential Information" in this Contract includes any information disclosed by Destination NSW or by its employees, agents and assigns to the Supplier either before or after the commencement of the Term that is confidential by its nature, is designated as confidential, specifically identified as confidential, or the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s know, or ought to know, is confidential. \nBoth parties may disclose Confidential Information to their need-to-know employees and agents and to their legal and professional advisors to the extent required for the performance of this Contract, subject to an ongoing obligation of confidentiality and provided that the disclosing party ensures that the recipient in turn keeps the Confidential Information confidential and does not use the information except for the purposes of this Contract. Your The Supplier\'s intended recipients must be pre-approved in writing by Destination NSW and you the Supplier will ensure that its permitted recipients of our Confidential Information execute a confidentiality deed, prior to receiving the Confidential Information, to protect Destination NSW, which deed must be in a form approved by Destination NSW. \nBoth parties must collect, use, and store any Personal Information in relation to the Services strictly in compliance with the Privacy and Data Protection Laws and: \nFor the purpose of the clause, Privacy and Data Protection Laws means the Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 (NSW) and if relevant to the performance of this Contract, the Health Records and Information Privacy Act 2002 (NSW), the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), the Spam Act 2003 (Cth), the General Data Protection Regulation (2016/679), and the Privacy and Electronic Communications Directive (2002/58/EC), including any principles under those Acts and codes of practice relating to those Acts.\nFor the purpose of this clause, "Personal Information" means the definition set out in the relevant Privacy and Data Protection Laws. \nImmediately on termination of this Contract for any reason, both parties must:\ncease the use of all Confidential Information of, or relating to, the other party;\ndeliver to the other party all documents and other materials in its possession or control containing, recording or constituting the other party?s Confidential Information, or, at the other party?s option, destroy, and certify to the other party that it has destroyed, those documents; \nfor other party Confidential Information stored electronically, permanently delete that Confidential Information from all electronic media on which it is stored, so that it cannot be restored.\nNothing in clause 35 above shall prevent Destination NSW from any record storage actions it is required to take pursuant to the State Records Act 1998 (NSW).  \nThe Supplier acknowledges that the restrictions in this clause are reasonable in all the circumstances and necessary to protect the assets and information of Destination NSW (including brand and reputation) and the remedy of damages may be inadequate to protect the interests of Destination NSW and Destination NSW is entitled to seek and obtain injunctive relief, or any other relief.\nRight of Access to Records and Information\nFor the purposes of assisting Destination NSW meet its obligations under the GIPA Act, the Supplier must, within seven (7) days of receiving a written request by Destination NSW, provide Destination NSW with immediate access to the following information contained in records held by the Supplier or its Key Person/s:\ninformation that relates directly to the provision to Destination NSW of the Goods or Services under this Contract;\ninformation collected by the Supplier from members of the public for the purposes of the provision of the Goods or Services under this Contract; and/or\ninformation received by the Supplier from Destination NSW to enable the Supplier to provide the Goods or Services under this Contract. \nFor the purpose of clause 38, information does not include:\ninformation that discloses or would tend to disclose financing arrangements, financial modelling, cost structure or profit margin;\ninformation that the Supplier is prohibited from disclosing to Destination NSW by provision made by or under any act, whether of any State or Territory, or of the Commonwealth; or\ninformation that, if disclosed to Destination NSW, could reasonably be expected to place the Supplier at a substantial commercial disadvantage in relation to Destination NSW, whether at present or in the future. \nThe Supplier will provide copies of any of the information requested by Destination NSW under clause 38 and any associated costs for this may be separately agreed between the parties. \nAny failure by the Supplier to comply with any request under clause 38 above will be considered a breach of an essential term and will allow Destination NSW to terminate this Contract by providing notice in writing of its intention to do so with the termination to take effect seven (7) days after receipt of the notice. If the Supplier fails to remedy the breach within the seven (7) day period to the satisfaction of Destination NSW, then the termination will take effect seven (7) days after receipt of the notice.\nIntellectual Property \nThe Supplier agrees that ownership of all intellectual property rights subsisting in any material created under this Contract, including but not limited to [insert specific description of relevant material under this Contract, or delete] plans, documents and photographs or footage (final and raw, proofs, negatives) shall vest in Destination NSW royalty free, for use worldwide, free of charge and in perpetuity. For the purpose of this clause, ?intellectual property? expressly excludes the Supplier?s pre-existing materials, and the Supplier retains all right, title and interest in and to any and all pre-existing materials. For the purposes of this Contract, the pre-existing materials are deemed to be the Supplier?s confidential information. To the extent any pre-existing materials are used in connection with the Services and/or incorporated or contained in any deliverable provided to Destination NSW pursuant to this contract, the Supplier hereby grants Destination NSW a non-exclusive, irrevocable, perpetual, royalty-free, worldwide license to use the pre-existing materials in accordance with this contract. The Supplier?s pre-existing materials include any software, tools, algorithms, know-how, ideas, techniques, materials, architecture, methodologies, concepts, trade secrets and other proprietary information that were owned or licensed by the Supplier prior to entering into this Contract. The Supplier warrants that Destination NSW?s exploitation of the intellectual property rights referenced in this clause, as contemplated under this Contract, will not infringe the intellectual property rights of any third party. \nDestination NSW intellectual property subsisting in any materials or documents provided by Destination NSW to the Supplier is the sole and exclusive property of Destination NSW or its licensors (as applicable) and this Contract does not confer on the Supplier any proprietary right, ownership, or title to any of the Destination NSW intellectual property. \nThe Supplier consents to and must ensure each of its employee(s) and contractor(s) consent to Destination NSW doing anything in respect of the intellectual property rights assigned or licensed to Destination NSW under this Contract which would otherwise breach the moral rights of the affected party granted to it by the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). \nDispute Resolution\nIf a dispute under this Contract arises (?Dispute?):\nthe party claiming that a Dispute has arisen must give a written notice to the other party (?Dispute Notice?), setting out reasonable particulars of the Dispute;\nwithin five (5) business days after receipt of a Dispute Notice, a senior representative of each party must meet and attempt to resolve the Dispute within 10 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice;\nif the parties fail to meet or resolve the Dispute within 10 business days of the receipt of the Dispute Notice, the chief executive officer (or equivalent) of each party (or his or her nominee) must meet (in person or by way of telephone conference) and attempt to resolve the Dispute within 20 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice; and\nif the parties fail to meet or resolve the Dispute within 20 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice, the parties may take whatever action they consider necessary to resolve the Dispute. \nCommunications between the parties during the process set out in clause 45, whether oral or in writing, will not be admissible as evidence in any legal process unless in writing and signed by both parties. \nDespite the existence of a Dispute, the parties must continue to perform their respective obligation under this Contract. \nNothing in clauses 45 to 47 prevents a party issuing proceedings from obtaining urgent injunctive or urgent declaratory relief. \nAssignment\nThe Supplier may, with Destination NSW?s approval, assign any rights or duties under this Contract or sub-contract performance of this Contract or any part of it. Provided the individual or entity the Supplier assigns or subcontracts any rights or duties to is qualified to perform the Services, Destination NSW?s approval will not be unreasonably withheld. Engaging sub-contractors to provide any part of the Services will not relieve the Supplier from any of its liabilities or obligations under this Contract. \nArtificial Intelligence Systems\nNotwithstanding any other provision of this Contract, each party (Party A) must not use or deploy an Artificial Intelligence System in the performance of this Contract, and is prohibited, without the other party?s (Party B) prior written consent, from reproducing, publishing, communicating, or otherwise using, in whole or in part:\nany intellectual property, confidential information or any other information of Party B; or \nany material created under this contract by either party; or\nor any trademarks of Party B; \n(collectively Party B Material),\nto develop, train or direct, Artificial Intelligence Systems, technology or models, including the mining or scraping of text, images or data from Party B Material that vests in whole or in part in Party B whether undertaken by the Party A or by third parties authorised, licensed or directed by Party A.\nArtificial Intelligence System means a model, or a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments.\nModern Slavery\nFor the purpose of this clause:\nCore Obligations means those obligations set out in clause 53 (Core Obligations);\nEngaged Entity of a party means any first tier (direct) suppliers, subcontractors, consultants and contractors engaged by that party (or that party?s directors, officers and employees) in connection with this Contract. For the avoidance of doubt, ?Engaged Entities? includes independent contractors (whether an individual or body corporate), secondees, consultants and any other workers (however described) who may be engaged for the purposes of this Contract but are not employed by the relevant party;\nManagement Plan means a plan to take reasonable steps to manage risks of Modern Slavery in the Supplier?s operations and supply chains (including in the operations and supply chains of Supplier?s Engaged Entities);\nMaterial Breach has the meaning given in clause 58;\nModern Slavery:\nmeans any conduct that constitutes or would constitute any offence listed in Schedule 2 of the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW), including an offence of attempting or incitement to commit such an offence;\nincludes any conduct that constitutes or would constitute an offence under any of the Modern Slavery Laws as amended from time to time, including an offence of attempting or incitement to commit such an offence; and\nincludes conduct engaged in elsewhere than in New South Wales that, if it occurred in New South Wales, would constitute a modern slavery offence under paragraphs (i) or (ii);\nModern Slavery Laws means:\nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (Cth);\nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW);\nDivisions 270 and 271 of the Commonwealth Criminal Code;\nsection 176(1A) of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912 (NSW);\nsection 438ZE of the Local Government Act 1993 (NSW); and\nany other laws, regulations, codes and international conventions aimed at combatting modern slavery, forced labour or human trafficking, from time to time in force in or ratified by Australia and, where relevant, in or by other jurisdictions in which the parties operate, \neach as amended from time to time;\nreasonable steps means those steps that are reasonable in the circumstances to prevent, identify, mitigate and remedy modern slavery. In assessing whether steps are reasonable, the parties may refer to the NSW Anti-slavery Commissioner?s Guidance on Reasonable Steps and related information and resources published by the Anti-slavery Commissioner;\nRelated Body Corporate has the meaning given to that term in the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth);\nRelated Entity means, in respect of a party, a Related Body Corporate of such party;\nRemediation Plan has the meaning given to it in clause 59. \nThe Supplier must:\nnot engage in Modern Slavery;\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its directors, officers, employees, Related Entities and Engaged Entities comply with Modern Slavery Laws as applicable;\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its Engaged Entities include provisions equivalent to the Core Obligations (including this sub-clause) in their contracts with their suppliers; and\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its Engaged Entities provide their respective directors, officers, employees and suppliers with at least the minimum level of wages and other entitlements required by law. \nThe Supplier acknowledges and agrees that the Contract Price supports the Supplier to comply with its Core Obligations.\nThe Supplier agrees that it will establish, implement, and maintain for the term of this Contract, appropriate systems and policies as required to meet its Core Obligations.\nWithout limiting the Core Obligations and clause 55, and to the extent permitted by law, the Supplier agrees that it will notify Destination NSW immediately with adequate particulars of the Modern Slavery and the actions taken, or being taken, to remedy the Modern Slavery if the Supplier becomes aware of any actual or reasonably suspected Modern Slavery engaged in, or any notices, investigations, proceedings or claims arising in any jurisdiction in relation to any actual or reasonably suspected breach of Modern Slavery Laws by the Supplier, the Supplier\'s directors, officers, employees, Related Entities, or by any of its Engaged Entities, whether or not the Modern Slavery occurs or is suspected to occur in the performance of the Contract.\nThe Supplier represents and warrants to, and for the benefit of, Destination NSW that, as at the Contract Date and on a continuing basis for the duration of the term of this Contract, the Supplier has disclosed, in accordance with clause 56:\nto the extent the Supplier is aware, any:\nactual or reasonably suspected Modern Slavery engaged in; and\nnotices, investigations, proceedings or claims arising in any jurisdiction in relation to any actual or reasonably suspected breach of Modern Slavery Laws,\nby the Supplier, he Supplier\'s directors, officers, employees, or Related Entities, or by any of the Supplier?s Engaged Entities while performing any contract with the Supplier, whether or not the Modern Slavery arises in the performance of the Contract; and\nall actions taken to remedy said Modern Slavery or breach of Modern Slavery Laws. \nWithout limiting the parties? rights under this Contract, the parties agree that:\na breach of the Supplier?s obligations under this clause will be taken to be a Material Breach of this Contract; and\nbefore exercising any termination rights that may arise as a result of this Material Breach, the parties shall consult with relevant stakeholders on whether Modern Slavery may arise from such termination and the reasonable steps to prevent or mitigate such risk of Modern Slavery. \nWhere Destination NSW forms the view that there is a Material Breach that is reasonably capable of being remedied, then the parties shall develop a remediation plan to take reasonable steps to remedy the breach in accordance with this Contract (the Remediation Plan). The Supplier shall take reasonable efforts to implement this Remediation Plan. \nPublic Interest Disclosures\nIn clauses 60 to 67, words and expressions:\nwhich are not defined in this Contract, but which have a defined meaning in the PID Act, have the same meaning as in the PID Act; and\nwhich are defined in this Contract but, by such a definition, are given a different meaning in other clauses of this Contract to the meaning given in the PID Act, take the same meaning as in the PID Act. \nThe Supplier must ensure that all individuals involved in providing services under this Contract are made aware of the following:\nthat those individuals are public officials for the purposes of the PID Act;\nhow to make a voluntary public interest disclosure;\nDestination NSW?s public interest disclosure policy; and\nthe fact that a person who is dissatisfied with the way in which a voluntary public interest disclosure has been dealt with may be entitled to take further action under the PID Act or another Act or law.\nThe Supplier must notify Destination NSW of a voluntary public interest disclosure of which the Supplier becomes aware where either:\nthe disclosure relates to Destination NSW; or\nthe maker of the disclosure is known to be a public official associated with Destination NSW.\nThe Supplier must notify Destination NSW of serious wrongdoing committed, or alleged to be committed, by an individual providing services under this Contract.\nThe Supplier must use its best endeavours to assist in an investigation of serious wrongdoing if requested to do so by a person dealing with a voluntary public disclosure on behalf of Destination NSW or any other agency (as defined in the PID Act).\nThe Supplier acknowledges that:\nDestination NSW has an obligation to take corrective action under the PID Act; and\nDestination NSW has a right to terminate the Contract in response to a finding of serious wrongdoing or other misconduct involving the Supplier or an individual providing services under this Contract.\nIf the Supplier subcontracts the Contract in whole or in part, the Supplier must ensure that the subcontract contains terms binding the person or body engaged under the subcontract that are equivalent to the terms binding the Supplier in clauses 60 to 67.\nThe parties acknowledge that the regulations made under the PID Act may make further provision about terms that must, or must not, be included in an agency service contract or a class of agency service contracts.\nEntire agreement and previous representations\nThis Contract states all the express terms of the Contract between the parties and supersedes all prior discussions and agreements in respect of its subject matter. \nUpon entering this Contract, the Supplier will be taken to have investigated and satisfied itself of the adequacy of all information relied upon by the Supplier to form a position in relation to the Goods or Services to be provided under this Contract. \nTo the maximum extent permitted by law, and other than as expressly specified in this Contract, Destination NSW does not:\nmake any representation or warranty (express or implied) as to the accuracy, completeness, currency, fitness for purpose, quality, reliability, usefulness or timeliness of the content of the information relied upon by the Supplier in entering this Contract to provide the Goods and/or Services or as to any of Destination NSW?s Confidential Information provided to the Supplier; or\nhave any liability whatsoever for any decision made by the Supplier or any other person to engage with Destination NSW to provide the Goods and/or Services. \nInsurance\nThe Supplier must take out and maintain the insurance cover identified at Item 10 of the Schedule with a reputable insurance company for the period of this Contract. Destination NSW may request confirmation of such cover by way of certificates of currency prior to the commencement of the Term. \nThe Supplier is also solely responsible for ensuring that any sub-contractors engaged are also suitably insured and acknowledges that it and its Key Person/s are not entitled to the benefit of any accident, third party, public liability or indemnity policies of insurance or any workers\' compensation policies which may be in force for the benefit or protection of Destination NSW staff. \nThe Supplier must roll over insurance policies as they expire to ensure coverage for the duration of the Term. If the Supplier fails to acquire adequate insurance or fail to renew its insurance policies during any roll over period within the Term, the Supplier must notify Destination NSW immediately.  Evidence of insurances, by way of certificates of currency, are to be provided to Destination NSW upon request.\nRelationship\nIt is agreed that the relationship created by this Contract is that of an independent contractor(s) and nothing contained in this Contract shall be deemed or construed as creating any partnership, joint venture, employment relationship, agency or other relationship between the parties or to make Destination NSW liable for the debts or obligations of the Supplier. Consequently, the Supplier agrees by entering into this Contract that they are:\nnot entitled to payment of salary, holiday pay, severance pay, long service leave, superannuation, ongoing employment or any other entitlement which an employee has in respect of their employer;\nsolely responsible for all payments in relation to taxes;\nsolely responsible for providing superannuation, leave and work cover in respect of its Key Person/s; and\nsolely responsible for complying with all relevant laws and regulations relating to the provision of this clause. \nConflicts of Interest\nThe Supplier must take reasonable steps to ensure that it (and its Key Person/s, employees, agents and assigns) are not subject to any conflict of interest that would adversely affect its ability to supply the Services to Destination NSW in accordance with the Contract or be competitive with Destination NSW or adversely impact upon Destination NSW\'s interests. Where there is potentially any conflict of interest, the Supplier must promptly inform Destination NSW. If the conflict of interest is of substantial concern to Destination NSW it may, in its absolute discretion, terminate this Contract under clause 20. \nDisrepute and no public comment\nThe Supplier agrees, covenants and warrants that it will not act in any way or manner which is, in the reasonable opinion of Destination NSW, prejudicial or otherwise detrimental to Destination NSW. The Supplier further agrees that it will not make any adverse, critical or disparaging statements, allegations or comments (including on social media) which disparage Destination NSW, the NSW Premier, the NSW Government or the Minister for Jobs and Tourism (or relevant portfolio Minister if there is a change to Ministerial title). The Supplier agrees to always use all reasonable efforts to preserve the goodwill and reputation of Destination NSW and comply with any reasonable requirement imposed by Destination NSW from time to time. The Supplier agrees it will use best endeavours to preserve and protect the value and validity of Destination NSW?s intellectual property, including by providing all necessary support, information, materials and assistance to enable Destination NSW to maintain and promote its image and reputation.\nUnless expressly agreed in writing by Destination NSW, the Supplier must not engage in any public comment or communication (including comments on radio, television, social media and digital platforms, comments to a journalist, and comments expressed in letters, newspapers, books, journals and notices) concerning the Services and your engagement by Destination NSW, or issue any public statement or communication or make any representation that in any way directly or indirectly affects Destination NSW or its affairs. Under no circumstances are there to be any media announcements (including exclusives) or information shared with the media without Destination NSW?s express approval. Furthermore, there are to be no media or press releases issued, including those under embargo, nor any pre briefing of any media.\nGeneral\n(Severability) If any part of this Contract is invalid, illegal or unenforceable, that part will be severed from this Contract without affecting the continued operation of the remainder of this Contract, and the rest of the Contract is enforceable except where enforcement would materially change the nature or effect of the parties? obligations under this Agreement.\n(Notices) Notices are to be sent by post, email or hand delivered to the attention of the respective persons referred to at Item 9 of the Contract Schedule at the address recorded there (or as notified in writing to the other party). \n(Jurisdiction) This Contract is governed by and in accordance with the laws in the State of New South Wales. The parties expressly submit to the exclusive jurisdiction of the courts in the state of NSW and courts of appeal from them. The Supplier irrevocably waives any objection to the venue of any legal process in these courts as being in an inconvenient forum. \n(Novation) Destination NSW may at any time require you to be a party to a novation of this Contract to any other NSW Government agency and you must consent to such novation and execute any document reasonably required by Destination NSW to give effect to it. \n(Counterparts) This Contract may be executed in any number of counterparts and scanned copies may be valid.\n(Survival) Clauses 20 to 25 inclusive (termination), 29 (indemnity), 31 to 48 inclusive (confidentiality), 38 to 41 inclusive (right of access to records), 42 to 44 inclusive (intellectual property), 45 to 48 inclusive (dispute resolution), 49 (assignment), 50 to 51 inclusive (artificial intelligence systems), 68 to 70 inclusive (entire agreement and previous representations), 76 (non-disrepute), 77 (no public comment), 80 (jurisdiction); 81 (novation) continue to operate and survive the termination or expiry of this Contract. Termination of this Contract does not affect the rights of Destination NSW against the Supplier in respect of anything done or omitted to be done by the Supplier under this Contract before termination or regarding any sums or other claims outstanding at the time of termination.\nANNEXURES\nThe documents below are subject to clauses 1 and 2 of the Contract Standard Terms and Conditions and form part of this contract:\nAnnexure\tDocument Description\nA\tSpecification of Services \nB\t\nC\t\nANNEXURE A ? SPECIFICATION OF SERVICES'}, {'file_name': '20260917-DNSW-Contract-of-Engagement-Template.txt', 'extracted_text': 'PARTY DETAILS\nParties\nDestination NSW\nLevel 2, 88 Cumberland Street, The Rocks, NSW, 2000\nABN: 52 890 768 976\n(Destination NSW or DNSW)\nand\n[Supplier name]\n[Insert registered address]\nABN/ACN/International Business Number \n(Supplier)\nCONTRACT SCHEDULE \nEXECUTION:\nCONTRACT STANDARD TERMS AND CONDITIONS\nGoods and Services\nFrom the Commencement Date until the Completion Date, the Supplier is engaged to undertake, supply and provide the goods and/or services, duties and outcomes set out in Item 3 and Item 4 of the Contract Schedule (?Goods and/or Services?). In exchange for the Goods and/or Services Destination NSW will pay the Supplier the Contract Price as set out in Item 6 of the Contract Schedule upon receipt of a valid invoice as set out in Item 7. \nEach party states that:\nit has full power and authority to execute, delivery and perform its obligations under this Contract;\nthis Contract is legally binding on and enforceable against it;\nit will comply with all applicable laws in the performance of its obligations under this Contract;\nits signatories are properly authorised; and\nit can enter into this Contract without breaching any other contract or legal obligation to anyone else.\nDestination NSW will reimburse the Supplier for out-of-pocket expenses in connection with Services, if any have been so identified for reimbursement in the invoice, provided that the Supplier has obtained prior written approval to incur such expenses at Destination NSW?s expense before they are incurred and valid tax invoices and receipts are provided for reconciliation purposes in accordance with Item 7. \nThe Supplier must provide the Goods and/or Services in accordance with any Milestones or Delivery Dates set out in Item 5 of the Contract Schedule. \nWhere the Supplier is supplying Goods to Destination NSW it:\nmust ensure and warrant that title to the Goods passes to Destination NSW on the Delivery Date free from encumbrances and all other adverse interests;\nacknowledges that all risk in the Goods will remain the Supplier\'s until the Goods are installed or delivered to the satisfaction of Destination NSW; and\nwarrants that the goods will be: \nfree from defects in materials or workmanship; \nof merchantable quality; \nfit for purpose; and,\ncarry any applicable manufacturer?s warranty which will pass to Destination NSW.\nThe Supplier and any Key Person/s must not unreasonably refuse a request by Destination NSW to inspect and test the Goods prior to acceptance of delivery of the Goods. Destination NSW is entitled to reject any Goods which are not manufactured or delivered in accordance with the terms of this Contract (which includes a failure to comply with the warranties contained in clause 5 (c) of this Contract). Destination NSW is not deemed to have accepted any Goods until the latter of:\nthe expiration of a reasonable time for inspection of the Goods following delivery and installation; or\nany latent defect in the Goods becoming apparent. \nIf any Goods are rejected by Destination NSW under clause 6, title to and risk in those Goods automatically re-vests in the Supplier. At the Supplier\'s sole expense, it is responsible for the collection and removal of the rejected Goods. Destination NSW may, in its sole discretion, require the Supplier to replace any rejected Goods, or reimburse the costs arising from having to acquire replacement Goods from a third party, without prejudice to any of the other rights or remedies Destination NSW may have under this Contract or at law. \nWhere the Supplier is providing Services to Destination NSW, the Supplier and any Key Person/s must:\nperform the Services in accordance with best industry practice. Destination NSW relies on the Supplier, as the Supplier of the Services, to know what the best industry practice is;\ncomply with the laws, standards and policies that apply to the Services;\nperform the Services with the due care, skill and diligence expected of a professional service supplier and in a proper and workmanlike manner; \nperform the Services in a cost-effective manner consistent with the required level of quality of performance; and\nensure that any equipment used in delivering the Services is suitable and in good, safe and serviceable order and continuously maintained or repaired so that it remains in such order.\nThe Supplier must notify Destination NSW immediately if it anticipates that there may be a delay in the provision of the Goods and/or Services. Any request for an extension of time to deliver the Goods and/or Services requires Destination NSW?s approval, which will not be unreasonably withheld. \nThe Supplier agrees that any work performed for Destination NSW will be deemed to fall within the Services for the Contract Price set out in Item 6 of the Contract Schedule. Should the Supplier wish to perform work believed to be outside the scope of the Services, it must seek written approval from Destination NSW before proceeding with any such work. Such approval may be withheld by Destination NSW in its sole discretion. \nTerm\nThis Contract will commence on the Commencement Date identified in Item 1 and will continue until the Completion Date identified in Item 1, unless renewed in accordance with clause 12.   \nThe Parties agree that Destination NSW may elect to renew this Contract for the Option Period(s) set out in Item 1 of the Contract Schedule, subject to the consent of the Supplier. \nIf Destination NSW elects to exercise an option to renew, Destination NSW will provide, within 30 days before the expiration of the Completion Date, notice in writing to the Supplier and the Contract Price Review mechanism set out in Item 8 of the Contract Schedule will apply. Unless otherwise agreed in writing between the parties (email is sufficient), all other terms and conditions set out in this Contract will remain in full force and effect.  \nInvoice and Tax\nThe Supplier must submit a tax invoice at the timeframes set out in Item 7 of the Contract Schedule. \nDestination NSW may withhold or apply a set-off against any tax invoice submitted by the Supplier if Destination NSW is not satisfied, acting reasonably, that the Goods and/or Services have been provided in accordance with the terms and conditions of this Contract. \nOther than as provided in this Contract, all taxes, duties and charges and taxation obligations imposed or levied in connection to the Contract Price (including, without limitation, making appropriate tax deductions and payments in respect of any Key Person/s as applicable) will be borne by the Supplier. \nIf GST is applicable under or in connection with this Contract, any reference in this clause to a term defined or used in the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (Cth) (?Act?) is, unless the context indicates otherwise, a reference to that term as defined or used in the Act. \nAny amount referred to in this Contract except as indicated otherwise, is expressed on a GST exclusive basis. The Supplier takes all responsibility for determining whether GST is applicable to it and Destination NSW will not be liable whatsoever, for upholding the Supplier\'s requirements and obligations under the Act. \nIf GST is payable under or in connection with this Contract, the consideration to be provided for the supply (unless it is expressly stated to include GST) is increased by an amount equal to the GST exclusive consideration multiplied by the rate at which GST is imposed in respect of the supply. \nTermination \nDestination NSW may at any time terminate this Contract for convenience by giving the Supplier 30 days? notice in writing, such termination being effective upon expiration of the 30-day period. Destination NSW will not be liable for any payments to the Supplier over and above the value of the payment due and payable for Goods provided or Services performed up to the date of termination under this clause. \nEither party may terminate this Contract if the other commits a material breach of this Contract which is either not capable of being remedied or which the party committing the breach has not remedied within seven (7) (or such larger number as agreed in writing) days of receiving a written notice requiring remedy from the other party. \nDestination NSW may terminate this Contract where the Supplier becomes bankrupt or stops, suspends or threatens to stop payment of all or a class of its debts, or where the Supplier appoints an administrator or liquidator.\nUpon termination of this Contract, any property belonging to Destination NSW which is in the possession of the Supplier and its Key Person/s must be immediately returned to Destination NSW.\nWhere this Contract is terminated by Destination NSW under clauses 21 or 22, Destination NSW may withhold any or all of the fees payable to the Supplier in exchange for the Services (other than fees for Goods or Services provided prior to the date of termination). Any fees prepaid by Destination NSW to the Supplier for Goods or Services not delivered to Destination NSW by the termination date are to be refunded to Destination NSW no later than 10 business days after the termination date.    \nIf there is any actual or threatened conduct which is or may be a breach of this Contract, both parties acknowledge that damages may be inadequate compensation for such a breach. Either party will be entitled to apply to any court of competent jurisdiction for interim or permanent injunctive relief or both, restraining the other party from committing any breach or threatened breach of this Contract. Such rights and remedies will be cumulative and in addition to any other rights or remedies which either party may be entitled to at law or in equity. \nForce Majeure\nA party (?Affected Party?) will not be liable for any failure or delay in the performance of its obligations under this Contract to the extent that such failure or delay is caused, directly or indirectly by any of the following circumstances (?Force Majeure Circumstance?):\nfire, flood, earthquake, or acts of God;\nstrikes or industrial action (but not strikes or industrial action by any employees of Destination NSW or the Supplier); and\nacts of war, pandemic, epidemic, terrorism, riots, civil disorders or rebellions or revolutions in Australia. \nThe Affected Party must:\npromptly advise the other party of the details of the Force Majeure Circumstance, and its likely effect on the Affected Party?s obligations under this Contract; and\ntake all steps reasonably necessary to recommence performance and minimise the delay caused by the Force Majeure Circumstance. \nIf the Services cannot be rearranged so that they are conducted, the Supplier will refund to Destination NSW within 10 business days of receiving notice from Destination NSW any unspent portion of the payments and no further payments will be made. \nIndemnity\nThe Supplier must indemnify Destination NSW against any loss, action, proceedings, demand, prosecution, judgment, damage, cost, expense, fine, penalty or liability claimed against or incurred by Destination NSW in relation to:\npersonal injury or damage to property arising out of:\na wilful misconduct (being an act or omission which was a deliberate and wrongful act or omission or involved reckless disregard or wanton indifference to the likely consequences) or any negligent act or omission by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, to provide the Goods or Services; or\nany breach by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s of this Contract (including but not limited to any loss resulting from the Supplier not being registered for GST and ABN purposes);\nbreach by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, of applicable Privacy and Data Protection Laws or confidentiality obligations; or\na material breach of the Contract by the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s, or infringement of Destination NSW?s intellectual property rights by the Supplier or its Key Peron/s; and,\nthe Supplier and/or its Key Person/s claiming that they are Destination NSW\'s employee, including claims for wages, superannuation, public holidays, annual leave, parental leave, personal carer s leave, long service leave, redundancy or termination of employment or any other employment entitlement. \nThe indemnity in this clause 29 will not apply to the extent that the relevant loss, action, proceedings, demand, prosecution, judgment, damage, cost, expense, fine, penalty or liability is caused or contributed to by Destination NSW.  \nKey Persons\nIf any Key Person/s are named at Item 2 of the Contract Schedule, then the Supplier must ensure that those Key Person/s are fully engaged in the Services on behalf of the Supplier. If any Key Person is unavailable, then the Supplier must notify Destination NSW and provide, at the earliest opportunity, replacements with comparable skill and experience, subject to written approval by Destination NSW. The Suppler acknowledges and agrees that Destination NSW may, in its absolute discretion, reject any proposed replacement(s) of one or more Key Person/s and that where this occurs, the Supplier must provide an alternate person with comparable skill and experience approved by Destination NSW of as soon as possible.\nConfidentiality, Personal Information and GIPA\nSubject to the Government Information (Public Access) Act 2009 (NSW) (GIPA Act), the Supplier and its Key Person/s acknowledge the importance of confidentiality in all aspects of Destination NSW?s operations and agree to keep all Confidential Information in absolute confidence and not to disclose any such information to any person without our prior written consent both during and after the term of this Contract. \n"Confidential Information" in this Contract includes any information disclosed by Destination NSW or by its employees, agents and assigns to the Supplier either before or after the commencement of the Term that is confidential by its nature, is designated as confidential, specifically identified as confidential, or the Supplier and/or its Key Person/s know, or ought to know, is confidential. \nBoth parties may disclose Confidential Information to their need-to-know employees and agents and to their legal and professional advisors to the extent required for the performance of this Contract, subject to an ongoing obligation of confidentiality and provided that the disclosing party ensures that the recipient in turn keeps the Confidential Information confidential and does not use the information except for the purposes of this Contract. Your The Supplier\'s intended recipients must be pre-approved in writing by Destination NSW and you the Supplier will ensure that its permitted recipients of our Confidential Information execute a confidentiality deed, prior to receiving the Confidential Information, to protect Destination NSW, which deed must be in a form approved by Destination NSW. \nBoth parties must collect, use, and store any Personal Information in relation to the Services strictly in compliance with the Privacy and Data Protection Laws and: \nFor the purpose of the clause, Privacy and Data Protection Laws means the Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 (NSW) and if relevant to the performance of this Contract, the Health Records and Information Privacy Act 2002 (NSW), the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), the Spam Act 2003 (Cth), the General Data Protection Regulation (2016/679), and the Privacy and Electronic Communications Directive (2002/58/EC), including any principles under those Acts and codes of practice relating to those Acts.\nFor the purpose of this clause, "Personal Information" means the definition set out in the relevant Privacy and Data Protection Laws. \nImmediately on termination of this Contract for any reason, both parties must:\ncease the use of all Confidential Information of, or relating to, the other party;\ndeliver to the other party all documents and other materials in its possession or control containing, recording or constituting the other party?s Confidential Information, or, at the other party?s option, destroy, and certify to the other party that it has destroyed, those documents; \nfor other party Confidential Information stored electronically, permanently delete that Confidential Information from all electronic media on which it is stored, so that it cannot be restored.\nNothing in clause 35 above shall prevent Destination NSW from any record storage actions it is required to take pursuant to the State Records Act 1998 (NSW).  \nThe Supplier acknowledges that the restrictions in this clause are reasonable in all the circumstances and necessary to protect the assets and information of Destination NSW (including brand and reputation) and the remedy of damages may be inadequate to protect the interests of Destination NSW and Destination NSW is entitled to seek and obtain injunctive relief, or any other relief.\nRight of Access to Records and Information\nFor the purposes of assisting Destination NSW meet its obligations under the GIPA Act, the Supplier must, within seven (7) days of receiving a written request by Destination NSW, provide Destination NSW with immediate access to the following information contained in records held by the Supplier or its Key Person/s:\ninformation that relates directly to the provision to Destination NSW of the Goods or Services under this Contract;\ninformation collected by the Supplier from members of the public for the purposes of the provision of the Goods or Services under this Contract; and/or\ninformation received by the Supplier from Destination NSW to enable the Supplier to provide the Goods or Services under this Contract. \nFor the purpose of clause 38, information does not include:\ninformation that discloses or would tend to disclose financing arrangements, financial modelling, cost structure or profit margin;\ninformation that the Supplier is prohibited from disclosing to Destination NSW by provision made by or under any act, whether of any State or Territory, or of the Commonwealth; or\ninformation that, if disclosed to Destination NSW, could reasonably be expected to place the Supplier at a substantial commercial disadvantage in relation to Destination NSW, whether at present or in the future. \nThe Supplier will provide copies of any of the information requested by Destination NSW under clause 38 and any associated costs for this may be separately agreed between the parties. \nAny failure by the Supplier to comply with any request under clause 38 above will be considered a breach of an essential term and will allow Destination NSW to terminate this Contract by providing notice in writing of its intention to do so with the termination to take effect seven (7) days after receipt of the notice. If the Supplier fails to remedy the breach within the seven (7) day period to the satisfaction of Destination NSW, then the termination will take effect seven (7) days after receipt of the notice.\nIntellectual Property \nThe Supplier agrees that ownership of all intellectual property rights subsisting in any material created under this Contract, including but not limited to [insert specific description of relevant material under this Contract, or delete] plans, documents and photographs or footage (final and raw, proofs, negatives) shall vest in Destination NSW royalty free, for use worldwide, free of charge and in perpetuity. For the purpose of this clause, ?intellectual property? expressly excludes the Supplier?s pre-existing materials, and the Supplier retains all right, title and interest in and to any and all pre-existing materials. For the purposes of this Contract, the pre-existing materials are deemed to be the Supplier?s confidential information. To the extent any pre-existing materials are used in connection with the Services and/or incorporated or contained in any deliverable provided to Destination NSW pursuant to this contract, the Supplier hereby grants Destination NSW a non-exclusive, irrevocable, perpetual, royalty-free, worldwide license to use the pre-existing materials in accordance with this contract. The Supplier?s pre-existing materials include any software, tools, algorithms, know-how, ideas, techniques, materials, architecture, methodologies, concepts, trade secrets and other proprietary information that were owned or licensed by the Supplier prior to entering into this Contract. The Supplier warrants that Destination NSW?s exploitation of the intellectual property rights referenced in this clause, as contemplated under this Contract, will not infringe the intellectual property rights of any third party. \nDestination NSW intellectual property subsisting in any materials or documents provided by Destination NSW to the Supplier is the sole and exclusive property of Destination NSW or its licensors (as applicable) and this Contract does not confer on the Supplier any proprietary right, ownership, or title to any of the Destination NSW intellectual property. \nThe Supplier consents to and must ensure each of its employee(s) and contractor(s) consent to Destination NSW doing anything in respect of the intellectual property rights assigned or licensed to Destination NSW under this Contract which would otherwise breach the moral rights of the affected party granted to it by the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). \nDispute Resolution\nIf a dispute under this Contract arises (?Dispute?):\nthe party claiming that a Dispute has arisen must give a written notice to the other party (?Dispute Notice?), setting out reasonable particulars of the Dispute;\nwithin five (5) business days after receipt of a Dispute Notice, a senior representative of each party must meet and attempt to resolve the Dispute within 10 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice;\nif the parties fail to meet or resolve the Dispute within 10 business days of the receipt of the Dispute Notice, the chief executive officer (or equivalent) of each party (or his or her nominee) must meet (in person or by way of telephone conference) and attempt to resolve the Dispute within 20 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice; and\nif the parties fail to meet or resolve the Dispute within 20 business days of receipt of the Dispute Notice, the parties may take whatever action they consider necessary to resolve the Dispute. \nCommunications between the parties during the process set out in clause 45, whether oral or in writing, will not be admissible as evidence in any legal process unless in writing and signed by both parties. \nDespite the existence of a Dispute, the parties must continue to perform their respective obligation under this Contract. \nNothing in clauses 45 to 47 prevents a party issuing proceedings from obtaining urgent injunctive or urgent declaratory relief. \nAssignment\nThe Supplier may, with Destination NSW?s approval, assign any rights or duties under this Contract or sub-contract performance of this Contract or any part of it. Provided the individual or entity the Supplier assigns or subcontracts any rights or duties to is qualified to perform the Services, Destination NSW?s approval will not be unreasonably withheld. Engaging sub-contractors to provide any part of the Services will not relieve the Supplier from any of its liabilities or obligations under this Contract. \nArtificial Intelligence Systems\nNotwithstanding any other provision of this Contract, each party (Party A) must not use or deploy an Artificial Intelligence System in the performance of this Contract, and is prohibited, without the other party?s (Party B) prior written consent, from reproducing, publishing, communicating, or otherwise using, in whole or in part:\nany intellectual property, confidential information or any other information of Party B; or \nany material created under this contract by either party; or\nor any trademarks of Party B; \n(collectively Party B Material),\nto develop, train or direct, Artificial Intelligence Systems, technology or models, including the mining or scraping of text, images or data from Party B Material that vests in whole or in part in Party B whether undertaken by the Party A or by third parties authorised, licensed or directed by Party A.\nArtificial Intelligence System means a model, or a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments.\nModern Slavery\nFor the purpose of this clause:\nCore Obligations means those obligations set out in clause 53 (Core Obligations);\nEngaged Entity of a party means any first tier (direct) suppliers, subcontractors, consultants and contractors engaged by that party (or that party?s directors, officers and employees) in connection with this Contract. For the avoidance of doubt, ?Engaged Entities? includes independent contractors (whether an individual or body corporate), secondees, consultants and any other workers (however described) who may be engaged for the purposes of this Contract but are not employed by the relevant party;\nManagement Plan means a plan to take reasonable steps to manage risks of Modern Slavery in the Supplier?s operations and supply chains (including in the operations and supply chains of Supplier?s Engaged Entities);\nMaterial Breach has the meaning given in clause 58;\nModern Slavery:\nmeans any conduct that constitutes or would constitute any offence listed in Schedule 2 of the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW), including an offence of attempting or incitement to commit such an offence;\nincludes any conduct that constitutes or would constitute an offence under any of the Modern Slavery Laws as amended from time to time, including an offence of attempting or incitement to commit such an offence; and\nincludes conduct engaged in elsewhere than in New South Wales that, if it occurred in New South Wales, would constitute a modern slavery offence under paragraphs (i) or (ii);\nModern Slavery Laws means:\nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (Cth);\nthe Modern Slavery Act 2018 (NSW);\nDivisions 270 and 271 of the Commonwealth Criminal Code;\nsection 176(1A) of the Public Works and Procurement Act 1912 (NSW);\nsection 438ZE of the Local Government Act 1993 (NSW); and\nany other laws, regulations, codes and international conventions aimed at combatting modern slavery, forced labour or human trafficking, from time to time in force in or ratified by Australia and, where relevant, in or by other jurisdictions in which the parties operate, \neach as amended from time to time;\nreasonable steps means those steps that are reasonable in the circumstances to prevent, identify, mitigate and remedy modern slavery. In assessing whether steps are reasonable, the parties may refer to the NSW Anti-slavery Commissioner?s Guidance on Reasonable Steps and related information and resources published by the Anti-slavery Commissioner;\nRelated Body Corporate has the meaning given to that term in the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth);\nRelated Entity means, in respect of a party, a Related Body Corporate of such party;\nRemediation Plan has the meaning given to it in clause 59. \nThe Supplier must:\nnot engage in Modern Slavery;\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its directors, officers, employees, Related Entities and Engaged Entities comply with Modern Slavery Laws as applicable;\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its Engaged Entities include provisions equivalent to the Core Obligations (including this sub-clause) in their contracts with their suppliers; and\ntake reasonable steps to ensure that its Engaged Entities provide their respective directors, officers, employees and suppliers with at least the minimum level of wages and other entitlements required by law. \nThe Supplier acknowledges and agrees that the Contract Price supports the Supplier to comply with its Core Obligations.\nThe Supplier agrees that it will establish, implement, and maintain for the term of this Contract, appropriate systems and policies as required to meet its Core Obligations.\nWithout limiting the Core Obligations and clause 55, and to the extent permitted by law, the Supplier agrees that it will notify Destination NSW immediately with adequate particulars of the Modern Slavery and the actions taken, or being taken, to remedy the Modern Slavery if the Supplier becomes aware of any actual or reasonably suspected Modern Slavery engaged in, or any notices, investigations, proceedings or claims arising in any jurisdiction in relation to any actual or reasonably suspected breach of Modern Slavery Laws by the Supplier, the Supplier\'s directors, officers, employees, Related Entities, or by any of its Engaged Entities, whether or not the Modern Slavery occurs or is suspected to occur in the performance of the Contract.\nThe Supplier represents and warrants to, and for the benefit of, Destination NSW that, as at the Contract Date and on a continuing basis for the duration of the term of this Contract, the Supplier has disclosed, in accordance with clause 56:\nto the extent the Supplier is aware, any:\nactual or reasonably suspected Modern Slavery engaged in; and\nnotices, investigations, proceedings or claims arising in any jurisdiction in relation to any actual or reasonably suspected breach of Modern Slavery Laws,\nby the Supplier, he Supplier\'s directors, officers, employees, or Related Entities, or by any of the Supplier?s Engaged Entities while performing any contract with the Supplier, whether or not the Modern Slavery arises in the performance of the Contract; and\nall actions taken to remedy said Modern Slavery or breach of Modern Slavery Laws. \nWithout limiting the parties? rights under this Contract, the parties agree that:\na breach of the Supplier?s obligations under this clause will be taken to be a Material Breach of this Contract; and\nbefore exercising any termination rights that may arise as a result of this Material Breach, the parties shall consult with relevant stakeholders on whether Modern Slavery may arise from such termination and the reasonable steps to prevent or mitigate such risk of Modern Slavery. \nWhere Destination NSW forms the view that there is a Material Breach that is reasonably capable of being remedied, then the parties shall develop a remediation plan to take reasonable steps to remedy the breach in accordance with this Contract (the Remediation Plan). The Supplier shall take reasonable efforts to implement this Remediation Plan. \nPublic Interest Disclosures\nIn clauses 60 to 67, words and expressions:\nwhich are not defined in this Contract, but which have a defined meaning in the PID Act, have the same meaning as in the PID Act; and\nwhich are defined in this Contract but, by such a definition, are given a different meaning in other clauses of this Contract to the meaning given in the PID Act, take the same meaning as in the PID Act. \nThe Supplier must ensure that all individuals involved in providing services under this Contract are made aware of the following:\nthat those individuals are public officials for the purposes of the PID Act;\nhow to make a voluntary public interest disclosure;\nDestination NSW?s public interest disclosure policy; and\nthe fact that a person who is dissatisfied with the way in which a voluntary public interest disclosure has been dealt with may be entitled to take further action under the PID Act or another Act or law.\nThe Supplier must notify Destination NSW of a voluntary public interest disclosure of which the Supplier becomes aware where either:\nthe disclosure relates to Destination NSW; or\nthe maker of the disclosure is known to be a public official associated with Destination NSW.\nThe Supplier must notify Destination NSW of serious wrongdoing committed, or alleged to be committed, by an individual providing services under this Contract.\nThe Supplier must use its best endeavours to assist in an investigation of serious wrongdoing if requested to do so by a person dealing with a voluntary public disclosure on behalf of Destination NSW or any other agency (as defined in the PID Act).\nThe Supplier acknowledges that:\nDestination NSW has an obligation to take corrective action under the PID Act; and\nDestination NSW has a right to terminate the Contract in response to a finding of serious wrongdoing or other misconduct involving the Supplier or an individual providing services under this Contract.\nIf the Supplier subcontracts the Contract in whole or in part, the Supplier must ensure that the subcontract contains terms binding the person or body engaged under the subcontract that are equivalent to the terms binding the Supplier in clauses 60 to 67.\nThe parties acknowledge that the regulations made under the PID Act may make further provision about terms that must, or must not, be included in an agency service contract or a class of agency service contracts.\nEntire agreement and previous representations\nThis Contract states all the express terms of the Contract between the parties and supersedes all prior discussions and agreements in respect of its subject matter. \nUpon entering this Contract, the Supplier will be taken to have investigated and satisfied itself of the adequacy of all information relied upon by the Supplier to form a position in relation to the Goods or Services to be provided under this Contract. \nTo the maximum extent permitted by law, and other than as expressly specified in this Contract, Destination NSW does not:\nmake any representation or warranty (express or implied) as to the accuracy, completeness, currency, fitness for purpose, quality, reliability, usefulness or timeliness of the content of the information relied upon by the Supplier in entering this Contract to provide the Goods and/or Services or as to any of Destination NSW?s Confidential Information provided to the Supplier; or\nhave any liability whatsoever for any decision made by the Supplier or any other person to engage with Destination NSW to provide the Goods and/or Services. \nInsurance\nThe Supplier must take out and maintain the insurance cover identified at Item 10 of the Schedule with a reputable insurance company for the period of this Contract. Destination NSW may request confirmation of such cover by way of certificates of currency prior to the commencement of the Term. \nThe Supplier is also solely responsible for ensuring that any sub-contractors engaged are also suitably insured and acknowledges that it and its Key Person/s are not entitled to the benefit of any accident, third party, public liability or indemnity policies of insurance or any workers\' compensation policies which may be in force for the benefit or protection of Destination NSW staff. \nThe Supplier must roll over insurance policies as they expire to ensure coverage for the duration of the Term. If the Supplier fails to acquire adequate insurance or fail to renew its insurance policies during any roll over period within the Term, the Supplier must notify Destination NSW immediately.  Evidence of insurances, by way of certificates of currency, are to be provided to Destination NSW upon request.\nRelationship\nIt is agreed that the relationship created by this Contract is that of an independent contractor(s) and nothing contained in this Contract shall be deemed or construed as creating any partnership, joint venture, employment relationship, agency or other relationship between the parties or to make Destination NSW liable for the debts or obligations of the Supplier. Consequently, the Supplier agrees by entering into this Contract that they are:\nnot entitled to payment of salary, holiday pay, severance pay, long service leave, superannuation, ongoing employment or any other entitlement which an employee has in respect of their employer;\nsolely responsible for all payments in relation to taxes;\nsolely responsible for providing superannuation, leave and work cover in respect of its Key Person/s; and\nsolely responsible for complying with all relevant laws and regulations relating to the provision of this clause. \nConflicts of Interest\nThe Supplier must take reasonable steps to ensure that it (and its Key Person/s, employees, agents and assigns) are not subject to any conflict of interest that would adversely affect its ability to supply the Services to Destination NSW in accordance with the Contract or be competitive with Destination NSW or adversely impact upon Destination NSW\'s interests. Where there is potentially any conflict of interest, the Supplier must promptly inform Destination NSW. If the conflict of interest is of substantial concern to Destination NSW it may, in its absolute discretion, terminate this Contract under clause 20. \nDisrepute and no public comment\nThe Supplier agrees, covenants and warrants that it will not act in any way or manner which is, in the reasonable opinion of Destination NSW, prejudicial or otherwise detrimental to Destination NSW. The Supplier further agrees that it will not make any adverse, critical or disparaging statements, allegations or comments (including on social media) which disparage Destination NSW, the NSW Premier, the NSW Government or the Minister for Jobs and Tourism (or relevant portfolio Minister if there is a change to Ministerial title). The Supplier agrees to always use all reasonable efforts to preserve the goodwill and reputation of Destination NSW and comply with any reasonable requirement imposed by Destination NSW from time to time. The Supplier agrees it will use best endeavours to preserve and protect the value and validity of Destination NSW?s intellectual property, including by providing all necessary support, information, materials and assistance to enable Destination NSW to maintain and promote its image and reputation.\nUnless expressly agreed in writing by Destination NSW, the Supplier must not engage in any public comment or communication (including comments on radio, television, social media and digital platforms, comments to a journalist, and comments expressed in letters, newspapers, books, journals and notices) concerning the Services and your engagement by Destination NSW, or issue any public statement or communication or make any representation that in any way directly or indirectly affects Destination NSW or its affairs. Under no circumstances are there to be any media announcements (including exclusives) or information shared with the media without Destination NSW?s express approval. Furthermore, there are to be no media or press releases issued, including those under embargo, nor any pre briefing of any media.\nGeneral\n(Severability) If any part of this Contract is invalid, illegal or unenforceable, that part will be severed from this Contract without affecting the continued operation of the remainder of this Contract, and the rest of the Contract is enforceable except where enforcement would materially change the nature or effect of the parties? obligations under this Agreement.\n(Notices) Notices are to be sent by post, email or hand delivered to the attention of the respective persons referred to at Item 9 of the Contract Schedule at the address recorded there (or as notified in writing to the other party). \n(Jurisdiction) This Contract is governed by and in accordance with the laws in the State of New South Wales. The parties expressly submit to the exclusive jurisdiction of the courts in the state of NSW and courts of appeal from them. The Supplier irrevocably waives any objection to the venue of any legal process in these courts as being in an inconvenient forum. \n(Novation) Destination NSW may at any time require you to be a party to a novation of this Contract to any other NSW Government agency and you must consent to such novation and execute any document reasonably required by Destination NSW to give effect to it. \n(Counterparts) This Contract may be executed in any number of counterparts and scanned copies may be valid.\n(Survival) Clauses 20 to 25 inclusive (termination), 29 (indemnity), 31 to 48 inclusive (confidentiality), 38 to 41 inclusive (right of access to records), 42 to 44 inclusive (intellectual property), 45 to 48 inclusive (dispute resolution), 49 (assignment), 50 to 51 inclusive (artificial intelligence systems), 68 to 70 inclusive (entire agreement and previous representations), 76 (non-disrepute), 77 (no public comment), 80 (jurisdiction); 81 (novation) continue to operate and survive the termination or expiry of this Contract. Termination of this Contract does not affect the rights of Destination NSW against the Supplier in respect of anything done or omitted to be done by the Supplier under this Contract before termination or regarding any sums or other claims outstanding at the time of termination.\nANNEXURES\nThe documents below are subject to clauses 1 and 2 of the Contract Standard Terms and Conditions and form part of this contract:\n\n\nANNEXURE A ? SPECIFICATION OF SERVICES\n'}, {'file_name': 'Part-C1---VS27-RFQ-Response-Schedule---Production-Manager---Music-Minds-Food.docx.txt', 'extracted_text': 'OFFICIALOFFICIAL\nOFFICIALOFFICIALRequest for Quotation\nOFFICIALOFFICIAL\nOFFICIALOFFICIAL\nOFFICIALOFFICIALPage 2 of 2\nOFFICIALOFFICIAL\nRFQ Title:\n13023050870\nProduction Manager ? Music, Minds, Food\nVivid Sydney 2027, 2028, 2029\nQuotation Non-Price Response \nSchedule 1 ? Quoter?s Details\nPlease provide your organisational details in the table below\n1.1 - Company Legal Name\t[insert details here]\n1.2 ? Company Trading Name\t[insert details here]\n1.3 - Australian Business Number (ABN)\tABN: [insert details here]ACN: [insert details here]\n1.4 - Company Registered Address\t[insert details here]\n1.5 - RFQ Contact Details\tName: [insert details here]Phone: [insert details here]Email: [insert details here]\n1.6 ? Registered for GST\tPlease confirm if your company is registered for Goods and Services Tax (GST)? Yes? NoIf ?No, please provide details below.\n1.7 - Validity Period\tHow long is your offer open for acceptance by DNSW? (Note: the minimum must be six months)\n1.7 - Validity Period\t[insert details to 1.7 here]\n1.8 - Addenda\tPlease indicate the number of Addenda received from DNSW and allowed for in your Response\n1.8 - Addenda\t[insert details to 1.8 here]\n1.9 - Classification\tPlease indicate if your organisation classified as one of the following:?  Small to Medium Enterprise ?  Regional Business?  Aboriginal Owned Business?  Disability Enterprise?  Large Organisation\n1.10 - Current Legal Proceedings\tAre you or any of your directors or close associates currently, or have you, or have your directors or close associates been at any time within the last five years, the subject of any or any pending:(a) legal proceedings, including winding up or bankruptcy proceedings,(b) insolvency administrations or investigations; and/or(c) investigations by ICAC or any other public body, including findings of dishonest, unfair and unconscionable conduct?? Yes? NoIf ?Yes?, please provide details below.\n1.11 - Insolvency\tPlease confirm if your company is not bankrupt or insolvent at the time of lodging the Tender Response? Yes? NoIf Yes, the entity may wish to consider the suitability of submitting a Tender Response, as DNSW will not contract with insolvent or bankrupt entities. \n1.12 - Conflict of Interests\tA conflict of interest exists when a reasonable person might perceive that your personal interests could be favoured over your professional duties. A conflict of interest also exists when it is likely that a person could be influenced, or could be reasonably perceived to be influenced, by a personal interest in carrying out their professional duties.Are you aware of any real or perceived conflict of interests (including any relevant relationships) existing, which require your disclosure?? Yes? NoIf yes, please provide details below.\n1.13 - Use of Confidential Information\tIn preparing a response, are you aware of your organisation using confidential information relating to any other party?? Yes? NoIf ?Yes?, please provide details below.\n1.14 - Compliance with Proposed Agreement?\tDo you agree to be bound by all the conditions contained within the Proposed Agreement?? Yes? NoIf ?No?, a full statement of deviations must be given below, specifying the relevant clause/s and the extent of non-compliance to each.\n1.15 Aboriginal owned business \tIs the Respondent an Aboriginal owned business recognised by? NSW Indigenous Chamber of Commerce;an organisation representing Aboriginal-owned businesses in another state or territory that is a member of\xa0the First Australians Chamber of Commerce and Industry; orSupply Nation? Yes? NoIf ?Yes, attach details.\n1.16 NSW Supplier Code 
